<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">East Timor</h1><p class="st-meta">2002 CE – present · capital Dili</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Young South-East Asian republic that won independence in 2002 after Portuguese rule and a brutal Indonesian occupation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Portugal withdrew from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-timor">Portuguese Timor</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia</a> invaded in December 1975 and annexed the territory. Its occupation cost an estimated 100,000 or more lives through killing, famine and disease. Resistance led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fretilin">Fretilin</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xanana-gusmao">Xanana Gusmao</a> continued, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-cruz-massacre">Santa Cruz massacre</a> of 1991 drew world attention. In a UN-run referendum in August 1999, 78.5 per cent voted for independence.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Pro-Indonesian militias then devastated the country until the Australian-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-force-east-timor">INTERFET</a> force arrived. After UN administration, the Democratic Republic of Timor-Leste became independent on 20 May 2002, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xanana-gusmao">Gusmao</a> as president. It is a semi-presidential democracy, overwhelmingly Catholic, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetum-language">Tetum</a> and Portuguese as official languages.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>The state survived a political crisis in 2006 and assassination attempts on its leaders in 2008. It depends heavily on offshore oil and gas revenue held in a petroleum fund, and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/association-of-southeast-asian-nations">ASEAN</a> in 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/east-timor.jpg" alt="East Timor" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: See File history, below, for details. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_East_Timor.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>2002 CE – present</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dili</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Xanana Gusmao, Jose Ramos-Horta, Mari Alkatiri</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Tetum, Portuguese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>East Timor</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Timor-Leste, Democratic Republic of Timor-Leste</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2014&amp;focus=east-timor" aria-label="East Timor on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8150 2497 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where East Timor lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8470 2753L8470 2753M8470 2753L7959 2664"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8470 2753h0M7959 2664h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8470 2753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8470 2753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Dili. <a href="/atlas?year=2014&amp;focus=east-timor">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
